{"authored" [{"question" "q-8309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013233/state.json" "file_sha256" "35d7d67a711e6e9e7b1e89ca7462dd3581856b6a9b88b39695ec1230c1c93fbe" "basis" 1164 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-8309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}=2C.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find ${C}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the above equation as\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013233/state.json" "file_sha256" "35d7d67a711e6e9e7b1e89ca7462dd3581856b6a9b88b39695ec1230c1c93fbe" "basis" 1164 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-8309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013233/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "afe84ab9c69899a72f3968d99750212c45d5146749e48d0ceaee86452fb5b9aa" "basis" 1164 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-8309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013233/state.json" "file_sha256" "35d7d67a711e6e9e7b1e89ca7462dd3581856b6a9b88b39695ec1230c1c93fbe" "basis" 1164 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-8309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013233/state.json" "file_sha256" "35d7d67a711e6e9e7b1e89ca7462dd3581856b6a9b88b39695ec1230c1c93fbe" "basis" 1164 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14013233/edb-import