rence/mathacademy/question-capture/14013233/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the particular solution to the differential equation\n$\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2}=0$\nthat satisfies the condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14028823/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d2448865aca91aecc19a4b1a66aa5a815bfc865917ae71a92e878d7e28c7c0" "basis" 1194 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14028823/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find the particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2{y}^{2} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-2{y}^{2} \\\\ -\\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ -\\int \\frac{1}{{y}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int 2\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{y} & =2x+C \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{2x+C}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the general solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{1}{2x+C},\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=2$ to find $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2 & =\\frac{1}{2(0)+C} \\\\ C & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the particular solution is\n\n$$\ny(x)=\\frac{2}{4x+1}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14028823/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d2448865aca91aecc19a4b1a66aa5a815bfc865917ae71a92e878d7e28c7c0" "basis" 1194 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14028823/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347853" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14028823/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "94228d1cf50b4ffe133be171eee2ad6084302ecd52a10d818f64b11428885957" "basis" 1194 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14028823/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-347853" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14028823/state.json" "file_sha256" "c5d2448865aca91aecc19a4b1a66aa5a815bfc865917ae71a92e878d7e28c7c0" "basis" 1194 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14028823/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-8309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the particular solution to the differential equation ${y}^{′}=\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)}$ that satisfies $y(0)=0.$ Assume that $-\\frac{\\pi }{4}<y<\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050314/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b083dbb847d3dfedf79fae411e6ca44f425417ff0dc1da3948e334a7aa2398e"} {"question" "q-8309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We first find the general solution and then apply the given initial condition to find a particular solution.\n\nSeparating variables and integrating both sides with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)} \\\\ (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) \\\\ \\int (\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y))\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2y)\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+C \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}\\,,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${C}_{1}=2C.$\n\nWe now apply the condition $y(0)=0$ to find ${C}_{1}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(0) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\cdot 0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3\\cdot 0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(0) & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)+{C}_{1} \\\\ 0 & =-\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot 1+{C}_{1} \\\\ {C}_{1} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we get the particular solution in implicit form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y) & =\\frac{2}{3}-\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can write the above equation as\n\n$$\n3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2y)=2(1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050314/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b083dbb847d3dfedf79fae411e6ca44f425417ff0dc1da3948e334a7aa2398e"} {"question" "q-8309" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050314/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "ba6835924838d81122aa90d9a5c26b5dec67f7b1a06cd80083dbfbf39543c9c6"} {"question" "q-8309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050314/state.json" "file_sha256" "1b083dbb847d3dfedf79fae411e6ca44f425417ff0dc1da3948e334a7aa2398e"} {"question" "q-8309" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3\\operato